      {
        "key": "b",
        "label": "(b)",
        "source": {
          "qp": 7
        },
        "stem": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "A new hand‑held technological device shows the main type of respiratory substrate being used in the cells of a person.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 7
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The device consists of a carbon dioxide sensor and air‑flow meter. The person inhales through the device for a fixed time and then exhales into it.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 7
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The device calculates the respiratory quotient (RQ) value to show whether the cells are mainly respiring carbohydrates or lipids.",
            "source": {
              "qp": 7
            }
          }
        ],
        "parts": [
          {
            "key": "b.i",
            "label": "(i)",
            "source": {
              "qp": 7,
              "ms": 8
            },
            "stem": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Explain how the device calculates the RQ value <b>and</b> how this shows whether the cells are mainly respiring carbohydrates or lipids.",
                "source": {
                  "qp": 7
                }
              }
            ],
            "marks": 4,
            "markScheme": {
              "marks": 4,
              "instruction": "<i>any </i><b><i>four</i></b><i> from: </i>",
              "guidance": [],
              "points": [
                {
                  "n": 1,
                  "text": "(RQ =)ratio of carbon dioxide (exhaled) to oxygen (inhaled)<b> ; </b>"
                },
                {
                  "n": 2,
                  "text": "air flow meter measures oxygen inhaled <b>; </b>"
                },
                {
                  "n": 3,
                  "text": "carbon dioxide sensor measures concentration of CO<sub>2</sub> exhaled <b>; </b>"
                },
                {
                  "n": 4,
                  "text": "carbohydrate RQ = 1 <b>; </b>"
                },
                {
                  "n": 5,
                  "text": "lipid RQ = 0.7 <b>; </b>"
                }
              ]
            },
            "er": [],
            "topics": [
              {
                "ref": "12.1(5)",
                "role": "primary"
              }
            ],
            "aiSupplement": {
              "explanation": [
                {
                  "text": "RQ compares carbon dioxide output with oxygen uptake over the same interval. The characteristic values arise from the different oxygen requirements of complete carbohydrate and lipid oxidation.",
                  "refs": [
                    "ms:b.i#1",
                    "ms:b.i#2",
                    "ms:b.i#3",
                    "ms:b.i#4",
                    "ms:b.i#5",
                    "syllabus:12.1(5)"
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "reasoningChain": [
                "Form the carbon dioxide to oxygen ratio.",
                "Use the carbohydrate and lipid reference values to identify the main substrate."
              ],
              "inferredMistakes": [
                {
                  "text": "Inverting the numerator and denominator of RQ.",
                  "basis": "inferred",
                  "refs": [
                    "ms:b.i#1",
                    "ms:b.i#2",
                    "ms:b.i#3",
                    "ms:b.i#4",
                    "ms:b.i#5",
                    "syllabus:12.1(5)"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "type": "extended"
          },
          {
            "key": "b.ii",
            "label": "(ii)",
            "source": {
              "qp": 7,
              "ms": 8
            },
            "stem": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "State the difference in the relative energy values of carbohydrates and lipids as respiratory substrates, <b>and</b> explain the reasons for the difference.",
                "source": {
                  "qp": 7
                }
              }
            ],
            "marks": 3,
            "markScheme": {
              "marks": 3,
              "instruction": "<i>any </i><b><i>three</i></b><i> from: </i>",
              "guidance": [],
              "points": [
                {
                  "n": 1,
                  "text": "lipid has higher energy (value) <b>OR</b><br> carbohydrate 15–17 kJ g<sup>-1</sup> andlipid 37–40 kJ g<sup>-1</sup> <b>; </b>"
                },
                {
                  "n": 2,
                  "text": "more, hydrogen (atoms) / C-H bonds, in lipid <b>; </b>"
                },
                {
                  "n": 3,
                  "text": "more reduced, NAD / FAD <b>; </b>"
                },
                {
                  "n": 4,
                  "text": "greater proton gradient / more protons in intermembrane space / more protons pass through ATP synth(et)ase <b>; </b>"
                }
              ]
            },
            "er": [],
            "topics": [
              {
                "ref": "12.1(4)",
                "role": "primary"
              }
            ],
            "aiSupplement": {
              "explanation": [
                {
                  "text": "Lipid contains more hydrogen per unit mass. Its oxidation therefore reduces more electron carriers, which can support a larger proton gradient and greater ATP production.",
                  "refs": [
                    "ms:b.ii#1",
                    "ms:b.ii#2",
                    "ms:b.ii#3",
                    "ms:b.ii#4",
                    "syllabus:12.1(4)"
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "reasoningChain": [
                "Compare energy released per gram.",
                "Link the hydrogen content to reduced carriers and oxidative phosphorylation."
              ],
              "inferredMistakes": [
                {
                  "text": "Comparing equal numbers of molecules instead of energy per unit mass.",
                  "basis": "inferred",
                  "refs": [
                    "ms:b.ii#1",
                    "ms:b.ii#2",
                    "ms:b.ii#3",
                    "ms:b.ii#4",
                    "syllabus:12.1(4)"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "type": "extended"
          }
